%!PS
% SPDX-FileCopyrightText: 2026 EfterScript contributors
% SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
% backend: none
% expect-output: F761046263646566FE6780>
% expect-output: FD61FD61FF610162630364656667FF6780>
% expect-output: aaaaaaaaaabcdefggg
% Scenario: RunLengthEncode writing through an ASCIIHexEncode filter to
% standard output, so the binary runs show as text. With record length
% 0 (no records) ten `a` become a repeat run (length byte 257 - 10 =
% 247, then the byte), `bcdef` a literal run (length byte 5 - 1, then
% the bytes), the final `ggg` a repeat run (257 - 3 = 254), and
% closefile ends the data with 128. With record length 4 no run crosses
% a record boundary: `aaaa`, `aaaa`, `aa` + `bc`, `defg`, `gg`.
% CloseTarget on the run-length filter closes the hexadecimal filter
% with it, which ends its own data with `>`. The first text decoded
% gives the bytes back.
% Expect: the two hexadecimal texts, then the decoded bytes.
(%stdout) (w) file /ASCIIHexEncode filter
<< /CloseTarget true >> 0 /RunLengthEncode filter
dup (aaaaaaaaaabcdefggg) writestring closefile
(\n) print
(%stdout) (w) file /ASCIIHexEncode filter
<< /CloseTarget true >> 4 /RunLengthEncode filter
dup (aaaaaaaaaabcdefggg) writestring closefile
(\n) print
<F761046263646566FE6780> /RunLengthDecode filter
100 string readstring pop =
